{"type":"say","text":"Thank you both for disclosing this critical information before I set the resolution package. Let me summarize what I'm working with: On the claimant's side, Daniel, you're telling me the former employee's counsel has indicated they will only accept a settlement if we change the personnel record to 'resigned in good standing' and provide a neutral reference limited to dates of employment and last job title. On the coverage side, Marisol, you're telling me the insurer has authorized exactly $95,000 inclusive of defense costs, with two conditions: mediation must be completed before execution of any settlement, and the insurer must provide written consent to the final settlement agreement. 

This creates a significant gap. The former employee demanded $180,000, and our insurer is only authorizing $95,000 inclusive of defense costs. I need to consider whether we can bridge that gap at mediation, whether there's room to negotiate the claimant down, and whether the company might need to consider funding any portion above the insurer's $95,000 authority if we want to resolve this. I also need to factor in the claimant's non-monetary conditions regarding the personnel record and reference language.

Before I finalize the resolution authority, I have a couple of follow-up questions. Daniel, is there anything else in the personnel file or HR records beyond what you've shared that could affect our leverage or exposure at mediation -- for example, any documentation supporting the performance-based termination, or anything that could undermine our position? And Marisol, does the insurer's $95,000 cap include only defense costs incurred to date, or does it also reserve for future defense costs if the case doesn't settle at mediation?"}

{"type": "say", "text": "Understood, Elena. To address your questions: On the flexibility of the $180,000 demand, I want to be straightforward -- during the exit-resolution call, the claimant's counsel did not indicate any specific flexibility or willingness to come down from the $180,000 figure. They framed the personnel record change and neutral reference as non-negotiable conditions, and the $180,000 was presented as the demand to resolve the claims. That said, this was a preliminary conversation, and counsel's position at mediation may differ once we're at the table. I don't want to overstate or understate what was communicated, so I'd characterize the $180,000 as the current demand without a clear signal of movement, but also without an explicit statement that it's a hard floor.

On the performance-based termination documentation, I'll review the file more thoroughly to assess whether the documentation is robust enough to support our position at mediation or whether there are any gaps that could undermine our leverage. I'll come back to you with that assessment before we reconvene. I agree it's important to weigh the precedent risk of agreeing to change the record to 'resigned in good standing,' especially if other terminated employees could point to it as a pattern.

I'll be ready to reconvene once Marisol has the defense cost breakdown and insurer's position on the non-monetary conditions."}

